# Taskmark file conventions

When creating or editing Taskmark markdown:

- Board layouts: 2.0 request boards keep `requests/<slug>/brief.md`,
  `requests/<slug>/epics/...`, `requests/<slug>/log/`, and `meetings/`;
  legacy boards keep `epics/`. On a request board new work goes under an
  existing request or a new request brief (`R-<slug>`, status `proposed` or
  `ready`); on a legacy board it stays under `epics/`.
- Accept legacy IDs like `T-297` and new IDs like `T-MM-a8f31c2d`.
- New IDs come from `scripts/allocate-id.py`; never use a shared counter.
- Static sizes only: XS=1, S=3, M=5, L=8, XL=13, XXL=21.
- Items have no estimate or owner property (only a request brief names an
  owner). Actual is derived only from closed leaf Work log intervals;
  parent Actual sums descendant leaves.
- Create writes new item files only. Relationships come from `parent` and
  `epic`; never rewrite parent child lists or rollups.
- Never edit an existing item file, request brief, request log entry, or
  meeting: no status, stages, dates, resolvers, checked criteria, Prompt &
  feedback, Work log, or Commits rows, no text edits. The Taskmark GitHub /
  Bitbucket App reconciles them from pushes and merged pull requests.
  `/tkmd-verify` is the only, maintainer-only exception.
- Execution changes product code only and never commits or pushes.
- Parent rollups, logs, status, people, and dates are read-time derived.
- Never generate `INDEX.md`, `SIZING.md`, `VELOCITY.md`, a generated board
  dashboard README, a README changelog, or Last synced metadata. `/tkmd-init`
  may write the static project README (not a dashboard).
- Board `CHANGELOG.md` is written only by `/tkmd-changelog` and
  `/tkmd-version`. Those commands do not commit.
- Follow the `taskmark-conventions` skill, including the board writing language.
